"""Run an ordered Unity-MCP sequence in ONE turn, reduced, stopping on the first failure.

    pf-mcp <<'EOF'
    assets-refresh               {}
    console-get-logs             {"count":20,"logType":"Error"}
    tests-run                    {"testMode":"EditMode","testAssembly":"Game.Tests.Editor"}
    editor-application-set-state {"state":"play"}
    screenshot-game-view         {}
    EOF

`testAssembly` is YOUR SKU's own EditMode test assembly — the framework spine names it
`Game.Tests.Editor`; read the real name off the `.asmdef` rather than copying this line.

Two costs are being attacked at once. An MCP result is pasted into context verbatim
and re-sent on every later turn, so a fat one is paid for the rest of the session —
the reducers cut it at the shell. And a fixed verify sequence is a script, not a
series of decisions, so it should cost one turn, not seven.

Unlike pf-exec this runs items IN ORDER, one at a time: the Editor is single
threaded, and later items depend on earlier ones. --stop-on-fail (default) means a
red compile never pays for the test run, the play-mode entry and the capture behind it.

`--timeout N` is SECONDS per item and is forwarded to the CLI (which takes milliseconds and
defaults to 60). The default here is 300, so a run that legitimately takes minutes — an EditMode
suite, a wide refresh — returns its real result instead of `Tool call timed out after 60 seconds`
while the Editor keeps working behind it.

**You never have to wait for the Editor by hand.** Every run probes it first and blocks until it
is free (`--wait-ready`, on by default; `--wait-ready-max` seconds, default 600). That probe is a
latency measurement, not a flag read — `IsCompiling` and `IsUpdating` are *false throughout* a
running test suite, so anything that reads them answers READY against an Editor that is a minute
from free. See the READY_* block in the source for the measurement. Consequences for the caller:

  * **Never write a `sleep` before a Unity call.** It is already handled, it returns sooner, and
    it cannot be wrong in the way a guessed number is wrong in both directions.
  * **A timeout is not a Unity failure.** A client abort does NOT abort Unity — the suite is still
    running inside a single-threaded Editor. Re-issue the same command: it now waits properly
    instead of landing on a busy Editor.
  * `pf-mcp: the Editor did not come free` means BUSY, not broken. Raise `--wait-ready-max`.

Two timeout ceilings, and the outer one is your host's: `pf-mcp --timeout 900` still dies at your
tool runner's own cap (two minutes by default in Claude Code's `Bash`) unless you ALSO raise that
tool's `timeout` parameter, or the project sets `BASH_DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS`. Setting only the inner
one reads as a Unity failure and costs the whole wait twice.

`tests-run` arguments are checked in the shell before Unity sees them:

  * `testClass` wants the SHORT class name. MCP matches it as `^.*\\.<class>\\.[^.]+$`, so a
    namespace-qualified `Game.Tests.Editor.MapScreenTests` can never match (nothing precedes the
    root namespace's dot) and came back `No tests found` — pf-mcp strips the namespace and says so.
  * An unknown key (`namespaceFilter`, `classFilter`, …) fails the item here, naming the real one,
    instead of costing a Unity round trip to learn the same thing.

A FULL `tests-run` (no testNamespace/testClass/testMethod) is skipped — reported `reused`, never
`ok` — when every input it can see is byte-identical to its last GREEN run: `Assets/`, `Packages/`,
`ProjectSettings/` and each `file:` package the manifest dev-links (hashed as git trees, ~0.2 s).
Measured on stories 7.2/7.3: 3 of 13 full EditMode runs (~10 min) re-ran a suite over a code tree
nothing had touched since a green run minutes earlier. `--rerun` forces it. The record lives in
`Library/pf-mcp/tests-run-stamps.json`; deleting `Library/` forgets it, which is the safe direction.

**A run stops starting new items once it has used its turn budget** (`--turn-budget`, seconds,
default 240; `0` turns it off). The items it did not start are written to a file and the run
ends with `INCOMPLETE … continue: Tools/pf/pf-mcp < FILE`, exit 1. Nothing was skipped for
cause; the batch is simply split across turns. The reason is the prompt cache. A subagent's
cache lives FIVE minutes. A Bash call that blocks longer than that lets it expire, and the next
turn re-writes the whole context at full write price. Measured on story 8.1: one stage agent ran
43 per-class `tests-run` items in a single call (729 s), and another call ran 341 s. Each came
back to a cold cache and re-wrote about 510k tokens, roughly $2.50 a time, about $5 for the two.
A turn resumed from a warm cache costs cents. The first item always runs, so one long item (a
whole-assembly suite) still gets its turn; only the items after it wait. Before an item starts,
the check adds the slowest item seen so far to the time already spent, so the budget is a real
ceiling and not a trigger that fires after the damage is done. An orchestrator whose cache lives
an hour loses almost nothing to the split. Pass `--turn-budget 0` only when you know the caller's
cache outlives the batch.

Reducers — per line, as a suffix after the JSON (`tests-run {...} --reduce raw`), or run-wide as
a CLI flag (`pf-mcp --reduce raw`); per-line wins. With neither, the default is chosen from the
tool name:
  logs-brief       LogType :: message[:250]        fail when an Error/Exception is present
  test-summary     failures only + the count line  fail when any test failed
  screenshot-path  writes the image, returns the PATH — never the pixels
  raw              whole payload, still capped
"""
import argparse, base64, json, os, re, subprocess, sys, tempfile, time

STATE = os.path.join(os.environ.get("TMPDIR", "/tmp"), "pf-mcp-playmode.json")
PLAYMODE_CEILING = 10          # PrototypeFramework: ten play-mode entries in one dev turn
CAP_DEFAULT = "2k"

# --- readiness probe ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What a busy Editor looks like, measured on a SKU project (Unity 6000.3.12f1) while a 482-test
# EditMode suite ran for 169 s, probing `editor-application-get-state` every 8 s:
#
#     t+  1.8s  1.8s  exit 0   IsCompiling=false IsUpdating=false
#     t+ 20.9s  1.6s  exit 0   IsCompiling=false IsUpdating=false
#     t+ 42.2s 13.2s  exit 0   IsCompiling=false IsUpdating=false
#     t+ 71.6s 21.5s  exit 1   (could not answer at all)
#     t+101.2s 21.5s  exit 1
#     t+130.6s 21.5s  exit 1
#     t+145.0s  6.3s  exit 0   IsCompiling=false IsUpdating=false
#     t+165.6s 12.7s  exit 0   IsCompiling=false IsUpdating=false
#
# Two facts fall out, and the second is the one that matters:
#
#   * THE FLAGS ARE BLIND. `IsCompiling` and `IsUpdating` were false at every single probe, all
#     the way through. A running test suite is not a compile and not an import, so reading those
#     fields to answer "is it still busy?" reads READY against an Editor that is 60 s from free.
#   * THE LATENCY IS THE SIGNAL. The probe costs 1.4-1.9 s against an idle Editor (~1.3 s of that
#     is npx starting) and 6-21 s, or a hard timeout, against a busy one. The Editor is single
#     threaded: the queue in front of a trivial call IS the work still to be done.
#
# So readiness is "answers, and answers FAST" — never a flag, and never a fixed sleep. 3.5 s sits
# in the empty band between the two clusters.
READY_PROBE = "editor-application-get-state"
READY_FAST = 3.5               # seconds: above this the answer came out of a queue, not an idle Editor
READY_PROBE_TIMEOUT = 20       # seconds per probe; a busy Editor simply never answers within it
READY_GAP = 5                  # seconds between probes


def human(n):
    return f"{n/1000:.1f}k" if n >= 1000 else f"{n}B"


def size(s):
    s = str(s).strip().lower()
    return int(float(s[:-1]) * 1000) if s.endswith("k") else int(s)


def payload(raw):
    """The CLI prints a banner before the JSON. Take the first value that parses."""
    dec = json.JSONDecoder()
    for i, ch in enumerate(raw):
        if ch == "{":
            try:
                return dec.raw_decode(raw[i:])[0]
            except ValueError:
                continue
    return None


def walk(node, want):
    """First value whose key matches `want`, anywhere in the payload."""
    if isinstance(node, dict):
        for k, v in node.items():
            if k.lower() in want and isinstance(v, (str, int, float)):
                return v
            hit = walk(v, want)
            if hit is not None:
                return hit
    elif isinstance(node, list):
        for v in node:
            hit = walk(v, want)
            if hit is not None:
                return hit
    return None


def results(d):
    """The per-entry list inside a payload, whatever wrapper the tool put it in.

    `tests-run` does NOT return a bare list: the real shape is
    {"result": {"Summary": {...}, "Results": [ {Name, Status, Message}, ... ]}}. Unwrapping only
    `structured`/`result` therefore returned [] for every test run, so `test-summary` printed the
    counts and NOTHING about which test failed — and the only way to a name was a second run of
    the whole suite under `--reduce raw`. Measured on story 7.1: that is the 165 s re-run this
    reducer exists to prevent, so the last hop looks inside a dict for the list it carries.
    """
    r = (d or {}).get("structured", d) or {}
    r = r.get("result", r) if isinstance(r, dict) else r
    if isinstance(r, dict):
        for k in ("Results", "results", "Entries", "entries", "Logs", "logs", "Items", "items"):
            if isinstance(r.get(k), list):
                return r[k]
        return []
    return r if isinstance(r, list) else []


# ─── reducers: (text, ok) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def red_logs(d, raw):
    rows = results(d)
    if not rows:
        return "0 entries", True
    out, bad = [], 0
    for e in rows:
        if not isinstance(e, dict):
            continue
        t = str(e.get("LogType") or e.get("logType") or "?")
        m = str(e.get("Message") or e.get("message") or "").replace("\n", " | ")[:250]
        if t.lower() in ("error", "exception", "assert"):
            bad += 1
        out.append(f"{t:9s} :: {m}")
    return "\n".join(out), bad == 0


def red_tests(d, raw):
    body = json.dumps(d) if d else raw
    # Unity-MCP returns PascalCase keys with a Tests suffix — {"TotalTests":467,"PassedTests":466,
    # "FailedTests":0}. The old pattern required the key to END at the word, so every EditMode run
    # reduced to "counts unavailable" and the caller re-ran the whole suite through the direct MCP
    # tool just to read pass/fail. Measured on story 7.1: two such re-runs, 165 s each.
    nums = {k.lower(): int(v)
            for k, v in re.findall(r'"?(passed|failed|skipped)(?:tests)?"?\s*[:=]\s*(\d+)', body, re.I)}
    fails = []
    for e in results(d):
        if not isinstance(e, dict):
            continue
        # The verdict key is `Status` in the real payload; `Result`/`result` are kept for the
        # shapes older CLI versions returned. Reading only the latter meant a red suite reduced to
        # a bare count — see results() above for what that cost.
        verdict = str(e.get("Status") or e.get("Result") or e.get("status") or e.get("result") or "").lower()
        if verdict in ("failed", "error"):
            name = e.get("Name") or e.get("FullName") or e.get("name") or "?"
            msg = str(e.get("Message") or e.get("message") or "").replace("\n", " | ")[:200]
            fails.append(f"FAIL {name}  {msg}")
    if not nums and not fails:
        # A run that reports no counts is UNSETTLED, never green (AD-14: check, don't assume).
        # Reporting ok here is how a suite that never executed reads as a pass.
        return "counts unavailable — rerun this item with `--reduce raw` and read the payload", False
    if not fails and not any(nums.values()):
        # 0 passed / 0 failed / 0 skipped means the FILTER matched nothing — a caller error, never
        # a pass. Same hazard as above: a run that executed no test must not read green.
        # This hint used to say testClass "wants the FULLY-QUALIFIED name" — the exact opposite of
        # MCP's `^.*\.<class>\.[^.]+$`, and every agent that obeyed it hit this line again.
        return ("0 tests ran — the filter matched nothing. Check testAssembly/testClass: testClass "
                "is the SHORT class name, testMethod the fully-qualified one"), False
    head = " / ".join(f"{v} {k}" for k, v in nums.items()) or "counts unavailable"
    ok = nums.get("failed", 1 if fails else 0) == 0 and not fails
    return "\n".join([head] + fails), ok


def red_shot(d, raw):
    """Never let pixels into context: persist the image, hand back the path."""
    p = walk(d, {"path", "filepath", "file", "savedto", "outputpath"})
    if isinstance(p, str) and os.path.exists(p):
        return f"{p} · {os.path.getsize(p)/1e6:.1f}MB", True
    b64 = walk(d, {"base64", "image", "imagebase64", "data", "content"})
    if isinstance(b64, str) and len(b64) > 512:
        blob = base64.b64decode(re.sub(r"^data:[^,]+,", "", b64), validate=False)
        dest = os.path.join(os.environ.get("TMPDIR", "/tmp"), f"pf-shot-{int(time.time())}.png")
        with open(dest, "wb") as fh:
            fh.write(blob)
        return f"{dest} · {len(blob)/1e6:.1f}MB  (read it only if you must look)", True
    return "no image field found — rerun with --reduce raw to inspect", False


def red_raw(d, raw):
    return (json.dumps(d, indent=1) if d else raw).strip(), True


REDUCERS = {"logs-brief": red_logs, "test-summary": red_tests,
            "screenshot-path": red_shot, "raw": red_raw}
AUTO = [(r"console-get-logs", "logs-brief"), (r"tests-run", "test-summary"),
        (r"^screenshot-", "screenshot-path")]


def pick(tool, explicit):
    if explicit:
        return explicit
    for pat, name in AUTO:
        if re.search(pat, tool):
            return name
    return "raw"


# ─── driver ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def parse(text):
    items = []
    for line in text.splitlines():
        line = line.strip()
        if not line or line.startswith("#"):
            continue
        reduce = None
        m = re.search(r"\s--reduce\s+([\w-]+)\s*$", line)
        if m:
            reduce, line = m.group(1), line[:m.start()].strip()
        tool, _, arg = line.partition(" ")
        items.append((tool.strip(), (arg.strip() or "{}"), reduce))
    return items


def cli(tool, arg, seconds):
    """One `unity-mcp-cli run-tool` invocation. Returns (exit_code, raw_text, elapsed_seconds).

    --timeout is forwarded to the CLI in MILLISECONDS, and that is the whole point of the flag:
    unity-mcp-cli's own default is 60_000 ms, so without this every call that legitimately runs
    longer than a minute — an EditMode suite, a big refresh — came back as `Tool call timed out
    after 60 seconds` while the Editor kept working, and the caller then guessed at a sleep. The
    subprocess cap sits ABOVE the CLI's so the CLI's own message wins the race and we never kill
    it mid-report.
    """
    t0 = time.time()
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["npx", "--yes", "unity-mcp-cli", "run-tool", tool,
                            "--timeout", str(int(seconds * 1000)), "--input", arg],
                           capture_output=True, timeout=seconds + 15)
        return p.returncode, (p.stdout + p.stderr).decode(errors="replace"), time.time() - t0
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return 124, f"pf-mcp: timed out after {seconds + 15}s", time.time() - t0
    except Exception as e:
        return 1, f"pf-mcp: {type(e).__name__}: {e}", time.time() - t0


def wait_ready(ceiling):
    """Block until the Editor answers a trivial call FAST. Returns (ready, waited, probes).

    This is the answer to the question a `sleep` guesses at. A client-side abort does NOT abort
    Unity — the Editor is single threaded and keeps running the suite — so the next call lands on
    a busy Editor and fails again. Measured on story 7.1: one 300 s abort, three retries into a
    busy Editor, then a blind `sleep 240` — eleven minutes spent on a suite that takes 2 m 40 s.

    A fixed sleep is wrong in both directions: too short and the retry fails anyway, too long and
    you buy five minutes for ninety seconds of work. This returns the moment the Editor is free
    and never earlier. See the READY_* block above for why latency, not a flag, is the signal.
    """
    t0 = time.time()
    probes = 0
    while True:
        code, _, took = cli(READY_PROBE, "{}", READY_PROBE_TIMEOUT)
        probes += 1
        if code == 0 and took < READY_FAST:
            return True, time.time() - t0, probes
        if time.time() - t0 >= ceiling:
            return False, time.time() - t0, probes
        time.sleep(READY_GAP)


# --- tests-run arguments ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Unity-MCP's own schema (Tests.Run.cs). Anything else is silently ignored by the tool, so a guessed
# key ran the WHOLE assembly or matched nothing — either way a Unity round trip to find out.
TESTS_RUN_KEYS = {"testMode", "testAssembly", "testNamespace", "testClass", "testMethod",
                  "includePassingTests", "includeMessages", "includeStacktrace", "includeLogs",
                  "logType", "includeLogsStacktrace"}
TESTS_RUN_ALIASES = {"namespaceFilter": "testNamespace", "classFilter": "testClass",
                     "methodFilter": "testMethod", "assembly": "testAssembly",
                     "namespace": "testNamespace", "class": "testClass", "method": "testMethod",
                     "mode": "testMode", "assemblyName": "testAssembly"}
TESTS_RUN_FILTERS = ("testNamespace", "testClass", "testMethod")


def check_tests_run(arg):
    """Return (arg, note, error) for one `tests-run` line.

    `testClass` is matched by MCP as the regex `^.*\\.<class>\\.[^.]+$` against each test's
    FullName (Tests.Run.cs, CreateClassRegexPattern). `^.*\\.` needs a dot BEFORE the class text, so
    `Game.Tests.Editor.MapScreenTests` never matches `Game.Tests.Editor.MapScreenTests.A_test` —
    nothing precedes the root namespace. Measured on stories 7.2/7.3: five such calls, each a
    `No tests found` and a retry. The namespace is DROPPED, not moved to `testNamespace`: MCP puts
    both into one `groupNames` list, which Unity ORs, so the pair would run the whole namespace.
    """
    try:
        d = json.loads(arg)
    except Exception:
        return arg, None, None                          # let Unity report malformed JSON itself
    if not isinstance(d, dict):
        return arg, None, None
    bad = sorted(k for k in d if k not in TESTS_RUN_KEYS)
    if bad:
        hints = [f"'{k}' (did you mean '{TESTS_RUN_ALIASES[k]}'?)" if k in TESTS_RUN_ALIASES
                 else f"'{k}'" for k in bad]
        return arg, None, ("pf-mcp: tests-run has no parameter " + ", ".join(hints) +
                           ". Its filters are testAssembly / testNamespace / testClass (short "
                           "name) / testMethod (fully qualified). Not sent to Unity.")
    note = None
    tc = d.get("testClass")
    if isinstance(tc, str) and "." in tc.strip("."):
        short = tc.strip(".").rsplit(".", 1)[-1]
        d["testClass"] = short
        note = (f"testClass '{tc}' -> '{short}' (MCP matches the short name; a namespace-qualified "
                f"one can never match)")
        arg = json.dumps(d, separators=(",", ":"))
    return arg, note, None


def is_full_run(arg):
    try:
        d = json.loads(arg)
    except Exception:
        return False
    return isinstance(d, dict) and not any(d.get(k) for k in TESTS_RUN_FILTERS)


# --- tests-run stamp -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
STAMP_FILE = os.path.join("Library", "pf-mcp", "tests-run-stamps.json")


def _git(args, cwd, env=None):
    return subprocess.run(["git"] + args, cwd=cwd, env=env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)


def _tree(repo, paths):
    """Content hash of `paths` in `repo` — tracked AND untracked (gitignore respected) — as a git
    tree id. A throwaway index seeded from the real one means unchanged files are stat-skipped,
    not re-hashed: ~0.2 s warm on a 9 MB-font project. None when anything about it fails."""
    import shutil, tempfile
    try:
        idx = _git(["rev-parse", "--git-path", "index"], repo).stdout.strip()
        idx = idx if os.path.isabs(idx) else os.path.join(repo, idx)
        fd, tmp = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix="pf-mcp-idx-")
        os.close(fd)
        try:
            if os.path.exists(idx):
                shutil.copyfile(idx, tmp)
            env = dict(os.environ, GIT_INDEX_FILE=tmp)
            if _git(["add", "-A", "--"] + paths, repo, env).returncode != 0:
                return None
            w = _git(["write-tree"], repo, env)
            return w.stdout.strip() if w.returncode == 0 else None
        finally:
            os.unlink(tmp)
    except Exception:
        return None


def inputs_stamp():
    """Everything a full EditMode run can see, or None (⇒ always run)."""
    try:
        root = _git(["rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"], os.getcwd()).stdout.strip()
        if not root:
            return None, None
        paths = [p for p in ("Assets", "Packages", "ProjectSettings") if os.path.exists(os.path.join(root, p))]
        parts = ["sku=" + (_tree(root, paths) or "")]
        manifest = json.load(open(os.path.join(root, "Packages", "manifest.json")))
        for name, ref in sorted(manifest.get("dependencies", {}).items()):
            if not str(ref).startswith("file:"):
                continue                                  # registry/git pins are in the lock file
            pkg = os.path.normpath(os.path.join(root, "Packages", ref[5:]))
            top = _git(["rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"], pkg).stdout.strip() if os.path.isdir(pkg) else ""
            if not top:
                return root, None                         # a dev-link we cannot hash: never skip
            parts.append(f"{name}=" + (_tree(top, [os.path.relpath(pkg, top)]) or ""))
        if any(p.endswith("=") for p in parts):
            return root, None
        return root, "|".join(parts)
    except Exception:
        return None, None


def stamp_key(arg):
    d = json.loads(arg)
    return f"{d.get('testMode') or 'EditMode'}|{d.get('testAssembly') or '*'}"


def stamp_load(root):
    try:
        return json.load(open(os.path.join(root, STAMP_FILE)))
    except Exception:
        return {}


def stamp_save(root, data):
    try:
        path = os.path.join(root, STAMP_FILE)
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        json.dump(data, open(path, "w"), indent=1)
    except Exception:
        pass                                              # a lost record only costs a re-run


def playmode_bump(tool, arg):
    if tool != "editor-application-set-state" or "play" not in arg.lower():
        return None
    try:
        n = json.load(open(STATE)).get("n", 0)
    except Exception:
        n = 0
    n += 1
    json.dump({"n": n, "at": time.time()}, open(STATE, "w"))
    if n > PLAYMODE_CEILING:
        return (f"play-mode entry #{n} — the framework budget is {PLAYMODE_CEILING} per dev turn. "
                f"Play mode is for looking, not sweeping. Reset: rm {STATE}")
    return None


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(add_help=True)
    ap.add_argument("--cap", default=CAP_DEFAULT, help=f"max bytes PER ITEM (default {CAP_DEFAULT})")
    ap.add_argument("--timeout", type=int, default=300,
                    help="per-item seconds, forwarded to unity-mcp-cli --timeout (default 300; "
                         "the CLI's own default is 60)")
    # `--reduce` is per-LINE by default (a suffix inside the heredoc). It is also accepted here as
    # a run-wide default, because guessing it as a CLI flag is the obvious mistake and it used to
    # exit 2 with `unrecognized arguments` — a wasted turn at full context.
    ap.add_argument("--reduce", choices=sorted(REDUCERS), default=None,
                    help="default reducer for every item that does not carry its own "
                         "`--reduce NAME` suffix (per-line always wins)")
    ap.add_argument("--stop-on-fail", dest="stop", action="store_true", default=True)
    ap.add_argument("--no-stop-on-fail", dest="stop", action="store_false")
    # ON by default, and deliberately so: the rule it replaces ("never answer a timeout with a
    # fixed sleep") was already written in this file's docstring seven hours before story 7.1
    # started, and that story still spent 800 s in four hand-written sleeps. A rule an agent must
    # remember is not a mechanism. Costs one ~1.4 s probe per run against an idle Editor.
    ap.add_argument("--wait-ready", dest="wait", action="store_true", default=True)
    ap.add_argument("--no-wait-ready", dest="wait", action="store_false",
                    help="skip the readiness probe (default: probe before the first item)")
    ap.add_argument("--wait-ready-max", type=int, default=600,
                    help="seconds to wait for a busy Editor before giving up (default 600)")
    ap.add_argument("--turn-budget", type=int, default=240,
                    help="seconds after which no NEW item is started; the rest are written to a file "
                         "to run in the next call, and the run exits 1 as INCOMPLETE. "
                         "Default 240 keeps a turn inside a subagent's 5-minute prompt cache; "
                         "0 disables")
    ap.add_argument("--rerun", action="store_true",
                    help="run a full tests-run even when its inputs match the last green run")
    a = ap.parse_args()

    t_start = time.time()
    items = parse(sys.stdin.read())
    if not items:
        sys.exit("pf-mcp: nothing on stdin. Feed it 'tool-name {json}' lines via a heredoc.")
    cap = size(a.cap)

    runs = [arg for tool, arg, _ in items if tool == "tests-run"]
    if len(runs) >= 2 or any(is_full_run(r) for r in runs):
        # Measured 2026-09-24: an unfocused Editor ticks ~6 Hz, so each tests-run here costs ~8-10 s
        # of fixed overhead, and a whole assembly hits the gateway's 300 s cap. pf-tests runs the
        # same classes as one synchronous run, in seconds.
        print("── hint: Tools/pf/pf-tests runs these classes in ONE synchronous run (seconds, not "
              "~10 s per class; no 300 s cap): `Tools/pf/pf-tests ClassA ClassB` or `--auto`")

    if a.wait:
        ready, waited, probes = wait_ready(a.wait_ready_max)
        if waited > READY_FAST * 2:                       # silent when the Editor was already free
            print(f"── wait-ready  {'READY' if ready else 'STILL BUSY'}"
                  f"  after {waited:.0f}s / {probes} probes")
        if not ready:
            # Running the batch anyway is how the 7.1 retry storm happened. Refuse instead, and
            # say what to do: the Editor is working, not broken, so the fix is more ceiling.
            sys.exit(f"pf-mcp: the Editor did not come free within {a.wait_ready_max}s "
                     f"({probes} probes). It is BUSY, not broken — a long suite or an import is "
                     f"still running. Raise --wait-ready-max, or --no-wait-ready to force the run.")

    total, nok, nfail, nskip = 0, 0, 0, 0
    stopped = False
    slowest = 0.0
    deferred = []
    for i, (tool, arg, reduce) in enumerate(items, 1):
        if (not stopped and a.turn_budget > 0 and i > 1
                and (time.time() - t_start) + slowest > a.turn_budget):
            deferred = items[i - 1:]
            break
        if stopped:
            print(f"\n── [{i}] {tool:30s} SKIPPED (--stop-on-fail)")
            nskip += 1
            continue

        warn = playmode_bump(tool, arg)
        t0 = time.time()
        note, stamp, stamp_root, reused = None, None, None, False
        if tool == "tests-run":
            arg, note, err = check_tests_run(arg)
            if err:
                print(f"\n── [{i}] {tool:30s} {'FAIL':10s} {human(len(err)):>7}   0.0s\n{err}")
                nfail += 1
                total += len(err)
                if a.stop:
                    stopped = True
                continue
            if is_full_run(arg):
                stamp_root, stamp = inputs_stamp()
                last = stamp_load(stamp_root).get(stamp_key(arg)) if stamp else None
                if stamp and not a.rerun and last and last.get("stamp") == stamp:
                    reused = True
                    text = (f"NOT RE-RUN — every input (Assets, Packages, ProjectSettings, dev-linked "
                            f"packages) is byte-identical to the last green run at "
                            f"{time.strftime('%H:%M:%S', time.localtime(last.get('at', 0)))}. "
                            f"That run: {last.get('summary', '?')}. --rerun to force.")
        if reused:
            ok = True
            code = 0
        else:
            code, raw, _ = cli(tool, arg, a.timeout)

        if reused:
            pass
        elif code != 0:
            text, ok = raw.strip()[:cap], False
        else:
            name = pick(tool, reduce or a.reduce)
            try:
                text, ok = REDUCERS[name](payload(raw), raw)
            except Exception as e:                        # a reducer must never mask the payload
                text, ok = f"pf-mcp: reducer {name} failed ({e}); raw head:\n{raw[:cap]}", False

        if stamp and not reused:
            rec = stamp_load(stamp_root)
            if ok:
                rec[stamp_key(arg)] = {"stamp": stamp, "at": time.time(),
                                       "summary": text.splitlines()[0][:160] if text else "?"}
            else:
                rec.pop(stamp_key(arg), None)             # a red run is never a baseline
            stamp_save(stamp_root, rec)
        if note:
            text = f"   note: {note}\n{text}"

        if len(text) > cap:
            text = text[:cap] + f"\n   [cut — rerun this item with --cap {human(len(text)*2)}]"
        total += len(text)
        nok, nfail = nok + ok, nfail + (not ok)

        status = "reused" if reused else ("ok" if ok else (f"exit {code}" if code else "FAIL"))
        print(f"\n── [{i}] {tool:30s} {status:10s} {human(len(text)):>7} {time.time()-t0:5.1f}s")
        if warn:
            print(f"   !! {warn}")
        print(text if text.strip() else "   (empty)")

        slowest = max(slowest, time.time() - t0)
        if not ok and a.stop:
            stopped = True

    rest = None
    if deferred:
        fd, rest = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix="pf-mcp-rest-", suffix=".mcp")
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as f:
            for tool, arg, reduce in deferred:
                f.write(f"{tool} {arg}" + (f" --reduce {reduce}" if reduce else "") + "\n")
    print(f"\n── {human(total)} (~{total//4} tok) / {len(items)} items"
          f" · {nok} ok · {nfail} fail · {nskip} skipped"
          + (f" · {len(deferred)} deferred" if deferred else ""))
    if deferred:
        # Flags that shape the run (timeout, reducer, stop-on-fail, rerun) carry over. The budget
        # does too: the next call is another turn with the same cache.
        flags = [f"--timeout {a.timeout}", f"--cap {a.cap}", f"--turn-budget {a.turn_budget}"]
        if a.reduce:
            flags.append(f"--reduce {a.reduce}")
        if not a.stop:
            flags.append("--no-stop-on-fail")
        if a.rerun:
            flags.append("--rerun")
        print(f"── INCOMPLETE: turn budget {a.turn_budget}s spent after {time.time() - t_start:.0f}s — "
              f"{len(deferred)} item(s) NOT run (not failed, not skipped for cause). Continue in the "
              f"next call:\n   Tools/pf/pf-mcp {' '.join(flags)} < {rest}")
    sys.exit(1 if (nfail or deferred) else 0)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
